\begin{align*}
2x + y &= \frac{114}{5} \\
2x + 3y &= \frac{290}{13} \\
\end{align*}

Multiplying the first equation by 3, we have
\begin{align*}
6x + 3y &= \frac{342}{5} \\
2x + 3y &= \frac{290}{13} \\
\end{align*}

Subtracting the second equation from the first equation, we have
\begin{align*}
4x &= \frac{342}{5} - \frac{290}{13} \\
4x &= \frac{576}{65} \\
x &= \frac{144}{65}
\end{align*}

Substituting this into the first equation, we have
\begin{align*}
2\left(\frac{144}{65}\right) + y &= \frac{114}{5} \\
\frac{288}{65} + y &= \frac{114}{5} \\
y &= \frac{114}{5} - \frac{288}{65} \\
y &= \frac{3516}{325} - \frac{1728}{325} \\
y &= \frac{1788}{325}
\end{align*}

Therefore,
\[\mathbf{v} = \begin{pmatrix} \frac{144}{65} \\ \frac{1788}{325} \end{pmatrix}\]
